sentences.l:
But, aiming through the throng, he did not escape Adamas,
Asius’-son, who stabbed the middle of his shield with sharp bronze
from close by, having rushed upon him;

and he, following, around the spear
writhed, as when an ox, which in the mountains cowherd men
with ropes, unwilling, by force having bound, lead along;

so he, struck, writhed for a little while, not very long at all,
until from his flesh the spear he pulled out, having come from close by,
the hero Meriones;

and he from under his shield took a fine
axe of good bronze, about a handle of olive wood,
long, well-polished;

Truly the one the ridge of the horse-haired helmet struck,
at the top under the crest itself, and the other, him coming on, the forehead
above the last part of the nose;

and the bones cracked, and his two eyes
beside his feet, bloody, fell to the ground in the dust,
and he doubled over, having fallen;

There upon him leapt the son of Pylaemenes the king,
Harpalion, who his dear father followed, to fight,
to Troy, and not again did he come to his fatherland;

And sitting down on the spot, in the hands of his dear companions,
breathing out his life, like a worm upon the earth
he lay, stretched out;

Him the Paphlagonians, great-spirited, busied themselves about,
and onto a chariot having lifted him they led him to sacred Ilium,
grieving;
